     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Kanazawa University researchers identify internal structural defects in microtubules</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers at Kanazawa University’s Nano Life Science Institute have discovered that tiny structural defects within microtubule walls can trigger their collapse. Using high-speed atomic force microscopy, the team observed these breakdowns even in the presence of paclitaxel, a common cancer drug designed to stabilize the structures.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-27/kanazawa-university-researchers-identify-internal-structural.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>These findings challenge the conventional scientific understanding that microtubule degradation occurs primarily at their ends. By revealing how internal damage initiates structural failure, this study offers new insights into cell division processes and why cancer cells may exhibit varying responses to chemotherapy treatments that target the cytoskeleton.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately summarizes the study's findings regarding microtubule wall defects and their challenge to conventional scientific models.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-scientists-hidden-weaknesses-microtubules-stabilized.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
