     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-27 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Mandhana Drops from Top ODI Batting Rank; Harmanpreet, Deepti Climb in Latest ICC Rankings</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#ICC Rankings</span> <span>#Smriti Mandhana</span> <span>#Harmanpreet Kaur</span></div>
       <p>India skipper Smriti Mandhana has relinquished her position at the top of the Women's ODI batting rankings, with South Africa captain Laura Wolvaardt reclaiming the No. 1 spot in the latest ICC update. Mandhana's dip in rankings follows a difficult start to the home series against Zimbabwe in Vadodara, where she managed just 32 runs in the opening match and was dismissed for a duck in the second ODI. Despite India having already clinched the three-match series by winning both completed games, Mandhana's back-to-back low scores led to her shedding valuable rating points.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-27/mandhana-drops-from-top-odi-batting-rank-harmanpreet-deepti-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>While Mandhana experienced a lean run, several other Indian players capitalized on their opportunities. Former India captain Harmanpreet Kaur advanced two places to eighth among ODI batters, and Deepti Sharma moved up four spots to 23rd in the same category, both after striking centuries in the second ODI against Zimbabwe.</p><p>Deepti Sharma's gains extended beyond batting; she also climbed two places to fourth in the ODI all-rounders' list and rose one spot to eighth among ODI bowlers, showcasing her comprehensive performance. India pacer Kranti Gaud also made progress, moving up two places to 25th in the ODI bowling rankings. The most striking individual leap among Indian players came from young batter Pratika Rawal, who jumped seven spots to reach a career-best 21st in the ODI batting charts.</p><p>Zimbabwean players also registered significant movements in the ODI batting rankings: Modester Mupachikwa climbed five places to 39th, Chipo Mugeri-Tiripano gained four spots to 47th, and Beloved Biza made the biggest jump among the trio, rising 16 places to 56th.</p><p>Indian players recorded further gains in the T20I rankings following the conclusion of their series against Zimbabwe. Shafali Verma advanced one place to third among T20I batters, while Mandhana herself climbed one spot to fourth in that format, despite her ODI setback. Elsewhere, Australia's Ash Gardner jumped nine places to 19th among T20I batters after the recent series against New Zealand. Australian bowlers Georgia Wareham and Kim Garth moved up to joint seventh and joint 10th, respectively, in the T20I bowling charts. From the New Zealand camp, Bree Illing rose six places to 17th among T20I bowlers, and Nensi Patel climbed to 29th following the Trans-Tasman series.</p><p>India's widespread gains across both formats underscore the depth of talent within the squad, even as Mandhana's ODI ranking dip serves as a reminder of how quickly standings can shift at the top.</p><p>What to watch: Mandhana's performance in upcoming series to regain her top ODI batting rank.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a well-structured, accurate summary that includes all specific ranking movements and context provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/smriti-mandhana-loses-no-1-odi-batting-rank-harmanpreet-and-deepti-rise/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
