     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-27 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Rashid Khan, Ijaz Ahmadzai power Afghanistan to 37-run win in 1st T20I vs Zimbabwe</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#T20I</span> <span>#Afghanistan</span> <span>#Zimbabwe</span></div>
       <p>Afghanistan secured a convincing 37-run victory over Zimbabwe in the opening T20I of their UAE series, played in Sharjah on October 27, 2026. Despite a challenging start that saw them reduced to 31 for 3 during the powerplay, Afghanistan recovered to post a total of 177 all out in 19.5 overs. In response, Zimbabwe was dismissed for 140 in 19.1 overs, giving the Blue Tigers an early 1-0 series advantage, Crictracker reported.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-27/rashid-khan-ijaz-ahmadzai-power-afghanistan-to-37-run-win-in.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Ijaz Ahmadzai's explosive batting proved to be a turning point for Afghanistan. Coming in after the top order's collapse, Ahmadzai smashed a blistering 51 runs off just 20 balls, including four fours and four sixes, decisively shifting the momentum. His aggressive knock, combined with a steady contribution from Shahidullah Kamal, helped Afghanistan recover from a precarious position and build a competitive total. Afghanistan reached the 50-run mark in 9.3 overs, accelerating sharply through the middle and closing stages.</p><p>Ahmadzai's innings featured a rapid 50-run partnership with Kamal, which arrived off just 24 balls. During this stand, Ahmadzai contributed 37 runs while Kamal added 12, providing a crucial platform after three wickets fell inside the powerplay. Kamal finished with 46 runs off 42 balls, including two fours and three sixes, complementing Ahmadzai's aggression. Rashid Khan then delivered a devastating cameo at the death, smashing 30 runs off just 10 balls with one four and four sixes, propelling Afghanistan to a total that proved beyond Zimbabwe's reach.</p><p>Chasing 178 for victory, Zimbabwe made a relatively composed start, reaching 38 for 1 at the end of the six-over powerplay. However, Afghanistan's bowlers maintained consistent pressure through the middle overs, leading Zimbabwe to slip to 56 for 3 by the 9.3-over mark as wickets fell regularly. Although Zimbabwe reached the 100-run mark in 14.4 overs, the required scoring rate continued to climb. Afghanistan held firm, dismissing Zimbabwe for 140 in 19.1 overs and sealing the 37-run win. Rashid Khan also contributed with two wickets with the ball.</p><p>What to watch: The second T20I as Zimbabwe aims to level the series.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes all provided match statistics and narrative details from the source text.</p>
