     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Researchers Develop SPINE to Track Elusive Neutrinos</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory researchers have introduced SPINE, a machine-learning program designed to reconstruct neutrino interactions from complex detector data. Neutrinos are nearly massless, abundant particles that interact weakly with matter, making them difficult to study.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-27/researchers-develop-spine-to-track-elusive-neutrinos.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>SPINE analyzes images from liquid argon time-projection chambers, such as those used in the Short-Baseline Neutrino program and the upcoming Deep Underground Neutrino Experiment. By learning from simulated imagery, the tool identifies particle paths and interactions, providing a precise reconstruction technique necessary to study subtle physics effects that may explain dark matter and the existence of matter.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the development and application of SPINE as described.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-spine-machine-trail-ghost-particles.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
