    </div>
  </div>
  <nav class="catrow"><span class="catgrp"><b>News</b><a href="/category/national">national</a><a href="/category/world">world</a><a href="/category/defence">defence</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Money</b><a href="/category/business">business</a><a href="/category/finance">finance</a><a href="/category/education">education</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Sports</b><a href="/category/sports">sports</a><a href="/category/cricket">cricket</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Culture</b><a href="/category/entertainment">entertainment</a><a href="/category/positive">positive</a><a href="/category/funny">funny</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Life</b><a href="/category/science">science</a><a href="/category/environment">environment</a><a href="/category/space">space</a><a href="/category/health">health</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Tech</b><a href="/category/tech">tech</a><a href="/category/ai">ai</a></span></nav></div><div class="gtr"><span>Read in your language:</span><div id="google_translate_element"></div></div><div class="wrap"><main id="content"><p class="crumb"><a href="/">← Front page</a> · <a href="/archive">Archive</a> · <a href="/category/sports">sports</a></p>
     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-10-27 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Saransh Jain's 13-Wicket Haul Powers Madhya Pradesh to Innings Victory Against Bihar</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Ranji Trophy</span> <span>#Saransh Jain</span> <span>#Madhya Pradesh</span></div>
       <p>India spinner Saransh Jain delivered a career-best match performance, claiming 13 wickets to dismantle Bihar and lead Madhya Pradesh to an innings and 174-run victory in their Ranji Trophy Group A match. The contest concluded inside two days on Tuesday. The 33-year-old off-spinner registered devastating first-innings figures of 7 for 14, followed by a six-wicket haul in the second dig, conceding just 49 runs. His match figures stood at 13 for 89, surpassing his previous first-class best of 9 for 71.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-27/saransh-jains-13-wicket-haul-powers-madhya-pradesh-to-inning.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Madhya Pradesh had declared their first innings at 407 for eight, resuming the day at 261 for three. Young Saksham Purohit, making his Ranji Trophy debut, contributed 132 runs, adding 19 to his overnight score of 113 before being bowled by left-arm spinner Aditya Raj. Captain Rajat Patidar continued his strong form, smashing 109 runs off 133 balls, including 13 boundaries, before being run out. This marked Patidar's second successive Ranji Trophy century this season, building on performances for India A.</p><p>Bihar's second innings faltered quickly, slipping to 25 for two. Despite Ayush Loharuka's unbeaten 76 off 66 balls, nine Bihar batters managed only single-digit scores as Jain tore through the lineup to secure the comprehensive victory for Madhya Pradesh.</p><p>In other Ranji Trophy action, Baroda amassed a mammoth 616 against defending champions Jammu &amp; Kashmir in Vadodara. The highlight was 21-year-old Priyanshu Moliya, who scored an unbeaten 321 off 492 balls, featuring 36 boundaries and a six. Moliya continued from his overnight 113 not out, punishing the J&amp;K bowlers for a second full day. Mahesh Pithiya also contributed with 102. J&amp;K were 6 without loss from four overs at stumps on day 2, trailing by 636 runs.</p><p>In Mumbai, Musheer Khan provided two crucial breakthroughs for Mumbai as Haryana reached 108 for two at stumps on day two, in response to Mumbai's 430. Haryana trailed by 322 runs in the first innings. Musheer, a left-arm spinner, accounted for both Haryana openers, captain Ankit Kumar (35) and Himanshu Rana (27), breaking their 61-run opening partnership. Musheer, who bowled with control and aggression, stated, &quot;I have to play a supporting role to Shams (Mulani) and Tanush (Kotian). I try to bowl in the same areas and stop the flow of runs. I focus on bowling my stock ball as much as possible.&quot;</p><p>Musheer also reflected on his own batting, having scored 109 on the first day. &quot;I am happy, but not satisfied. I could have easily scored a 200. This is our home ground and we know how to bat here. You are recognised as a batter in Mumbai cricket only if you convert your starts into hundreds. Fifties aren't good enough,&quot; he commented. Mumbai's total of 430 was bolstered by Tanush Kotian's unbeaten 52. Former India captain Suryakumar Yadav was also present in the Mumbai dressing room.</p><p>Elsewhere, Tamil Nadu conceded a 19-run first-innings lead to Railways but fought back to reduce them to 107 for nine in their second innings, giving Railways an overall lead of 126 runs. R Sai Kishore took 4/38, and Jeganathan Hemchudeshan claimed 3/21. Chandigarh posted a massive 685 for five declared against Himachal Pradesh, riding on opener Nehal Pajni's 222, alongside half-centuries from Manan Vohra (61), Arslan Khan (99), Akshit Rana (74), and Bhagmender Lather (50 not out). Himachal Pradesh were 49 without loss in reply. In Dehradun, Uttarakhand conceded a 78-run first-innings lead to Rajasthan.</p><p>What to watch: The continuation of these Ranji Trophy matches and individual player performances.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report captures all key matches and statistics accurately from the provided source material.</p>
