     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-10-27 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Severe Air Quality Grips Indian Metros; Cyclone Arnab Threatens Andhra Pradesh; Other Major Events Unfold</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Air Quality</span> <span>#Cyclone</span> <span>#Legal</span> <span>#Security</span></div>
       <p>Air quality readings across India have reached worrying levels as pollution season begins, extending the crisis beyond the National Capital Region. Peak air quality index (AQI) recordings show Delhi at 353, Ghaziabad at 354, Sonipat at 373, Gurugram at 352, Faridabad at 344, and Noida at 262. Southern cities are also affected, with Chennai touching 326 and Bengaluru reaching 214. Globally, five of the top 10 most polluted cities are in India, including Sri Ganganagar, Delhi, Udaipur, Bengaluru, and Faridkot, with 13 out of the top 20 also being Indian cities.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-27/severe-air-quality-grips-indian-metros-cyclone-arnab-threate.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Meanwhile, Cyclone Arnab has intensified into a very severe cyclonic storm over the Bay of Bengal, moving northwest towards the Andhra Pradesh coast. Landfall is anticipated on October 28 between Yanam and Visakhapatnam, with wind speeds projected to reach between 120 and 150 kilometres per hour. The India Meteorological Department has issued a red alert for coastal districts such as Kakinada, Machilipatnam, Yanam, Visakhapatnam, and Srikakulam, along with parts of Odisha. The Andhra Pradesh government has declared holidays for schools and colleges in vulnerable districts and allocated 27 crore rupees for emergency response efforts. Authorities are conducting large-scale evacuations of low-lying and flood-prone areas, prioritizing elderly residents and pregnant women. Rescue teams from the National Disaster Response Force (NDRF) and State Disaster Response Force (SDRF) have been deployed, while the Armed Forces, Navy, and Coast Guard remain on standby. Precautionary flood-prevention measures are also underway in neighbouring Tamil Nadu and Puducherry.</p><p>A major security incident occurred in Tamil Nadu where 17 inmates staged a jailbreak at a prison in Theni. The prisoners feigned illness, then overpowered guards upon entry to their cell, seizing their keys before escaping. Five inmates have since been recaptured, but 12 remain at large. Multiple search teams have initiated an extensive manhunt across neighbouring areas and border checkpoints. Following the breach, nine prison staffers have been suspended. The incident has raised concerns regarding prison administration, with reports highlighting overcrowding, as the facility housed 334 inmates against a capacity of 200, and questions about protocol adherence during security emergencies.</p><p>In legal developments, the Bombay High Court has granted interim relief to actor Imran Khan, directing that no coercive action be taken against him until November 2. This order follows a plea by Khan challenging a Foreigners Regional Registration Office Leave India Notice, which had instructed him to exit the country by October 28. The notice was issued after an FIR was registered at Gamdevi Police Station concerning his participation in an unlawful assembly protesting against CEC Gyanesh Kumar in Mumbai. The complaint was filed by BJP leader Kirit Somaiya. The Centre, represented by ASG Anil Singh, assured the court that the directive would remain in abeyance until the next hearing on November 2.</p><p>Internationally, voting is underway across Israel, with voter turnout exceeding 16 per cent in the initial hours, marking the highest rate recorded at this stage since 1981. Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and his wife, Sara, cast their votes at a polling station in Jerusalem. Netanyahu commended the voter turnout while urging right-wing voters to participate. Ground reports from a Jerusalem polling station showed voters queuing to cast ballots using party-marked slips placed inside envelopes. A local voter highlighted national unity, employment, and technology as key considerations for youth, rather than political division.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes all provided source material, maintaining the facts and structure of the report.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/video/severe-aqi-in-delhi-ncr-toxic-air-across-metros-5-indian-cities-in-world-top-10-ytvd-3026193-2026-10-27?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
