     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Finds Senate Election Forecasts Lack Historical Data</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers from UC Davis and George Mason University argue that election forecasts are less certain than they appear due to a lack of historical data on how national mood impacts individual Senate races. Their study, published in PS: Political Science &amp; Politics, highlights that Senate outcomes are increasingly driven by national forces rather than incumbency or state-level politics.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-27/study-finds-senate-election-forecasts-lack-historical-data.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Analyzing 269 races from 2010 to 2024, the team found that limited election cycles make it difficult to quantify how national tides move individual contests. Consequently, models often produce widely varying predictions, emphasizing the need for wider uncertainty intervals in forecasting.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately captures the study's findings on forecasting limitations and the role of national mood.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-election-lack-historical-national-mood.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
